\description{
These functions are the interface \code{frmtmb.sample} is written
against. They were internal until the sampling surface moved to its
own package, and they are documented here as one contract rather
than one page each, because what has to be reviewed is the set.

They are lower-level than the rest of frmtmb: they take assembled
frames, resolved prior entries and single random-effect blocks, not
formulas. Most users want \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}}, \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}} and
\code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} instead. Nothing here validates its
arguments the way the user-facing surface does.
}
\section{The objective seam}{

\code{build_objective(frame)} returns the bare negative log-likelihood
closure of an assembled frame, ready for \code{RTMB::MakeADFun()}. It
honors two fields a caller may set on a COPY of the frame:
\code{frame$map}, and \code{frame$ncp_blocks}, the indices of the
random-effect blocks to build in their non-centered form
(\verb{b = L(theta) z}). A prior-augmented objective is this closure plus
\code{neg_log_prior_fn()}'s.

\code{row_lpdf(fam, y, yobs, dpars, aterms, extra)} is the per-row
log-density composition the objective itself runs, with \code{cens()} and
\code{trunc()} folded in; it runs on numeric dpar values as readily as on
the tape, which is what makes a pointwise \code{log_lik()} reproduce the
fitted density exactly instead of approximating it.
\code{with_cs_offsets(fit, rspec, dpv)} takes one response spec from
\code{fit$spec$responses} and the dpar-value list \code{eval_dpars()} returns
for that response, and gives back the same list with the
category-specific (\code{cs()}) offsets applied; on a model without
\code{cs()} terms it returns \code{dpv} unchanged, so it is safe to call
unconditionally before \code{row_lpdf()}. \code{us_chol_cor(theta, K)} is the
unstructured correlation matrix of a \code{thetar} segment, which a
\code{set_rescor(TRUE)} model's joint row density needs.
}

\section{The prior seam}{

The prior VOCABULARY - \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}}, \code{\link[=prior_normal]{prior_normal()}} and its
relatives, \code{\link[=get_prior]{get_prior()}}, \code{\link[=prior_summary]{prior_summary()}} - is ordinary exported
API and is not part of this page. What is here is the RESOLUTION
machinery underneath it: \code{as_priorlist()} coerces the accepted
spellings (including a brms \code{brmsprior}) to one,
\code{resolve_prior_input()} turns a priorlist plus a model into
\code{list(entries, lower, upper)} on the internal parameter scale,
\code{neg_log_prior_fn()} turns resolved entries into a tapeable closure,
\code{resolve_bounds()} turns user-spelled bounds into internal-scale
vectors over the outer parameters, \code{spec_target()} names the slot
one specification addresses, and \code{spec_spelling()} gives back the
class and dpar it was WRITTEN with, which for a density on a
distributional parameter itself is that parameter's own class rather
than the intercept slot the resolver assigns to.

\code{vcov_estimated(fit)} is the covariance of EVERY estimated
coefficient under the internal names, which is what pairs with
\code{estimated_coef_names()}; \code{vcov()} itself covers brms's
population-level block and leaves out an intercept-only
distributional parameter. It does NOT cover an ordinal fit's
thresholds or its \code{cs()} coefficients, which live outside \code{beta}
and \code{betad}: the interop seams pair with a longer vector that has
them, and \code{vcov(fit, full = TRUE)} is where they are under
\code{confint()}'s names.

\code{fam_is_category_valued(fam)} says whether a response is a set of
categories, so that a predictive summary is a set of proportions
rather than a mean, and \code{predict_category_props(fit, rspec, draws)}
builds brms's \code{P(Y = k)} matrix from a matrix of simulated or
sampled responses. \code{brms_summary_matrix()}, \code{brms_summary_array()},
\code{brms_summarize_draws()} and \code{brms_prob_cols()} build brms's
\code{Estimate} / \code{Est.Error} / \code{Q} columns from a point estimate with a
standard error, or from draws. \code{brms_fixef_rows(fit)} gives brms's
population-level rows in brms's order: which coefficient-table rows
they are, and the ordinal thresholds and \code{cs()} coefficients that are
not, each with the map from the internal vector to the reported value,
so a draws object can report the same rows \code{fixef()} does.

\code{frmtmb_register_prior_defaults()} is the other direction: it lets a
package tell \code{\link[=get_prior]{get_prior()}} what defaults it would apply.
\code{get_prior()} reads the registry under \code{route = "sample"} ONLY, and
refuses that route when nothing is registered. Its default
\code{route = "fit"} reports what \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}} applies and reads nothing, so a
registration cannot change the answer of a call that did not ask
about the sampling route.
}

\section{The covstruct block readers}{

Each takes ONE element of \code{frame$re_blocks} and answers one
structural question about it. \code{ncp_eligible()} says whether the
block has a linear Cholesky factor and only standard-deviation and
correlation parameters, so that \verb{b = L(theta) z} is a bijection;
\code{ncp_scale_b()} and \code{ncp_unscale_b()} are that map and its inverse.
\code{covstruct_has_chol()} is the first half of eligibility on its own
(a factor is registered at all), \code{block_sd_idx()} gives the block's
standard-deviation positions within its theta segment,
\code{block_n_cor()} counts its correlation positions, \code{block_cor_prior()}
says whether an LKJ density fits them (\code{"none"}, \code{"lkj"} or
\code{"unsupported"}), and \code{is_student_block()} reports a Student-t
latent, which is a scale mixture and so has no linear factor at all.

The registry these read is deliberately NOT exported: exporting it
would make every field of every structure's entry into API, and
these eight questions are the ones anything outside covstruct.R has
ever needed to ask.
}

\section{The simulator seam}{

\code{sim_can()} and \code{sim_note()} say whether a family can be simulated
from and what is missing when it cannot; \code{sim_context()} builds the
context one draw is taken from and \code{sim_draw()} takes it.
\code{sim_is_structured()} reports whether the draw walks a fitted
structure - a hidden state sequence, a group-level latent class, a
correlated residual - which is what makes \code{newdata} and \code{re_formula}
refusable rather than merely unimplemented on a predictive method.
}

\section{brms's names and brms's VarCorr}{

\code{brms_coef_names(fit)} gives brms's name for each estimated
coefficient, in \code{estimated_coef_names()} order (\code{b_Intercept},
\code{b_sigma_Intercept}, or \code{sigma} for a sigma with no formula); \code{brms_re_rnames(fit, bk)} gives brms's
coefficient names for one random-effect block (\code{Intercept},
\code{sigma_Intercept}), and \code{brms_block_has_r(bk)} says whether the
block's sampled values are brms's \code{r_} coefficients.
\code{brms_coef_table(fit)} adds, per coefficient, whether brms reports it
as the distributional parameter itself on the natural scale (\code{sigma})
with its \code{linkinv} and \code{linkfun}; \code{brms_stan_name()} is brms's
response spelling (\code{y_a} is \code{ya}), \code{brms_group_name(bk)} its group
spelling, \code{brms_levels(bk, for_r)} its level spelling, and
\code{brms_re_parts(fit, bk)} a block's prefixes and renamed coefficients;
\code{brms_par_labels(fit, include_random)} labels a
sampled parameter vector in template order, in brms's spelling where
brms has a parameter with the same content. \code{varcorr_matrices(fit, theta)} is the covariance matrix of every block at \code{theta},
\code{varcorr_layout(fit)} is brms's \code{VarCorr()} grouping for the fit,
and \code{varcorr_values(fit, vals, comp, layout)} gives the standard
deviations, correlations and covariances of that layout at one
parameter vector in the \code{hyp_vals_only()} layout.
}

\section{Parameter labeling and fitted quantities}{

\code{par_name_bare()} is the draws-side spelling of a parameter name,
with parentheses dropped (\code{Intercept}, not \code{(Intercept)});
\code{outer_par_names()} names the outer parameter vector in
\code{\link[=confint]{confint()}} row order; \code{estimated_coef_names()} names the estimated
coefficient vector with mapped \code{betad} entries excluded;
\code{log_sd_theta_index()} says which theta positions are log standard
deviations, so that a variance at its boundary can be told from an
AR(1) coefficient at its own. \code{sdr_of()} is the cached
\code{TMB::sdreport()}, and \code{require_fitted()} is the refusal a
maximum-likelihood-only method owes an object that was assembled but
never optimized.
}

\section{The hypothesis expression engine}{

\code{hyp_parse_all()} turns hypothesis strings into expressions and
per-expression directions, once. \code{hyp_vals_only(fit)} reads the
fit's current estimates into a flat named value vector plus a
parallel component vector (which template component each value came
from); \code{hyp_env_vals(fit, vals, comp)} takes exactly that pair and
builds the evaluation environment, and \code{hyp_eval()} evaluates one
expression in it (\code{hyp_eval_in(ex, values)} over any named list keyed
by parameter name, and \code{hyp_expr_vars(ex)} the names it reads) - so a
caller with many parameter vectors parses
once, then per vector swaps the estimates in and calls
\code{hyp_vals_only()} and \code{hyp_env_vals()} again.
\code{hyp_tail_p()} is the tail probability of a directional claim.
\code{hyp_class_prefix(class, group)} is brms's name prefix for its
\code{class} and \code{group} arguments. \code{hyp_labels()} writes brms's
\code{Hypothesis} label for each string, \code{hyp_samples_frame(m, k)} lays a
draws matrix out as brms's \code{samples} frame (\code{H1}, \code{H2}, ...), and
\code{hyp_brms_result()} assembles the brms-shaped list every
\code{hypothesis()} method returns, so no method builds that shape on its
own.
}

\section{The conditional-effects engine}{

\code{ce_grids_build()} builds the prediction grids, effect list,
condition sets and base values; \code{ce_boot_one()} evaluates one grid
at one parameter vector and flattens it; \code{ce_frame()} lays out one
grid's rows in the column order brms's frame carries (the varied
predictor(s), every other model variable at its held value,
\code{cond__}, \code{cats__}, \code{effect1__}, \code{effect2__}), and \code{ce_finalize()}
assembles the per-effect data frames into the returned object with
the attributes \code{plot()} reads. \code{ce_cats_display()} says whether the
display is per-category and \code{ce_display_kind()} which of the three
displays a call asks for, \code{ce_structure_check()} is the refusal a
structured likelihood owes a grid, and \code{ce_re_formula()} resolves
the random-effect argument of such a call.

\code{ce_pred_dpar()} says what to PREDICT, which is not always the dpar
the display is labeled with. It returns \code{NULL} - the expected
response - whenever no dpar was named and the family's mean is not
the inverse link of \code{mu}, or the response carries \code{trunc()} bounds.
Call it rather than passing a resolved \code{dpar} straight to
\code{ce_boot_one()}: on a zero-inflated fit the mu predictor alone is
\code{1 / (1 - zi)} times the mean, on a mixture it is one component's
mean, and on a response truncated below at zero it can be negative.
The predicate underneath stays private, because what an extension
needs is the decision and not the test that makes it.

\code{ce_dots()} is the argument surface itself: it pulls
\code{allow_new_levels} out of a call's dots, returns whether it was set,
and REFUSES whatever is left, naming it. An extension that
hand-rolls the same check accepts a different set of arguments from
the fit method, which is how \code{allow_new_levels} came to work on a
fit and warn on draws.

Four more serve one purpose between them: making an unobserved
group a DRAWN group rather than letting the first observed one
stand in for it silently. \code{ce_group_vars()} names
the grouping variables a \code{re_formula = NULL} call blanks in its
grid; \code{ce_new_level_spec()} reads the blocks that blanking reaches;
\code{ce_boot_grids()} puts a placeholder level back in the grid the
design has to map; and \code{ce_draw_new_levels()} overwrites that
level's coefficients with one draw from the covariance the passed
object's own \code{theta} implies. A sampler calls the last one per
POSTERIOR DRAW where the fit method calls it per bootstrap
replicate; the construction is the same and the frames stay
comparable.
}

\section{The two-dialect argument seam}{

frmtmb answers to two argument dialects: a brms-named function takes
\code{re_formula}, frmtmb's own fit surface takes lme4's \code{re_formula}, and
the brms-named ones accept both. \code{arg_unset()} is the "not supplied"
marker a formal defaults to when \code{NULL} and \code{NA} are both real
settings and neither can double as unset; \code{re_form_arg()} resolves
the pair, refusing rather than guessing when both are given.
}

\section{The borrowed-generic seam}{

An extension that DEFINES a generic under a name another package
owns breaks that package. \code{UseMethod()} reads the method table of
the namespace where the generic it reached was defined, so the
extension's generic, reached first on the search path, sends the
owner's objects into a table with no method for them. Delayed
registration with \code{S3method(pkg::generic, class)} does not prevent
this: it puts the extension's method in the owner's table, and it
does nothing about the extension's rival generic.

\code{frm_install_generics(pkgname, owners)} is the repair frmtmb applies
to itself. \code{owners} is a named list with one entry per generic name,
each a character vector of the packages that own that name, in the
order to prefer when more than one is loaded and none is attached.
Each name becomes an active binding that returns the owner's generic
while the owner is loaded, and the package's own generic while it is
not. Call it FIRST in the extension's own \code{.onLoad()}, with the
extension's \code{pkgname}: the binding can only be installed while the
namespace is unsealed, which is during that call and at no later
time.

Three rules come with it. The extension's own generic must be a bare
\code{UseMethod()}, because whenever the owner is loaded that generic is
not the one that runs. Its formals must match the owner's. And every
method must be registered twice, with \code{S3method(generic, class)} for
the package's own table and \code{S3method(owner::generic, class)} for
each owner's, or it is unreachable while that owner is loaded.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(60), g = factor(rep(1:6, 10)))
dd$y <- rnorm(60, 1 + 0.5 * dd$x + rnorm(6, 0, 0.5)[dd$g], 1)
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd)

# the objective seam: the bare likelihood closure of the frame
nll <- build_objective(fit$frame)
nll(fit$estimates)

# a block reader: this one has a diagonal factor and one sd, so it
# can be sampled non-centered
bk <- fit$frame$re_blocks[[1]]
c(eligible = ncp_eligible(bk), n_cor = block_n_cor(bk),
  cor_prior = block_cor_prior(bk))

# parameter labeling, in the two spellings
head(outer_par_names(fit))
head(par_name_bare(outer_par_names(fit)))
}
